  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ec/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_48.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "We meet Alex Gentry in an Army Ordnance Center, who is admiring the natural defenses of the porcupine.&nbsp; He's making a porcupine suit for use by soldiers in combat.&nbsp; Shooting with gas-powered quill tubes requires a gas mask.&nbsp; He is mad that he won't be rewarded for creating it, and tired of being just another \"unknown, unsung scientist,\" so he decides to keep the suit for himself and test it on the newly-opened \"burglar-proof\" bank vault he reads about in the paper, a vault whose lock was designed by Henry Pym.\n\nThe next day, Hank and Jan are on hand to observe the opening of the new bank, but Jan is feeling under the weather.&nbsp; Without warning, Porcupine shows up, disables the guns of the nearby police with a shot of \"liquid cement,\" and sprays the crowd with sleeping gas.&nbsp; During the ensuing commotion, the Porcupine uses a smoke screen to hide his movements and uses a super-hot acetylene flame to burn through the bank vault lock.\n\nJan falls victim to the sleeping gas while Hank tries desperately to reach a window -- to break it so that fresh air gets in.&nbsp; But this just gives the Porcupine time to complete his heist, and soon, he's made off with a fortune in cash.&nbsp; By the time Jan wakes up, and she and Hank have become Wasp and Ant-Man, respectively, Porcupine has already fled the scene -- they're <i>too late</i>.\n\nBut Jan is still feeling woozy, both from a bout of the flu and from the gas, forcing Hank to rush her home to bed atop \"Torne,\" one of his faithful flying ants.&nbsp; She loves the fact that Hank is constantly having to hold her in his arms, even saying that it's worth being ill for all attention she's getting from him, and yet, all the while, Hank keeps calling her \"kid\" (and attributes her amorous comments as nothing more than the delirium talking).\n\nHank can't understand why she keeps bringing up how nice it is that he's even calling her \"dear,\" especially now that the Porcupine is at large.&nbsp; Jan is put in bed, and Hank contacts his ants to be on the lookout for the Porcupine.&nbsp; For her part, Jan points out that, \"I'll bet if <i>I</i> had six legs, you'd like me better!\"&nbsp; Jan finally drifts off and gets a good night's sleep.\n\nMeanwhile, emboldened by his first success, the Porcupine arrives the next morning at <i>another</i> bank and uses his weaponry to keep the depositors and guards at bay.&nbsp; But this time, his eventual exit and change back to Alex Gentry is observed by Hank's ant spies.&nbsp; Now aware of the villain's hiding place, Ant-Man decides to let Jan sleep before rushing out toward the Army Ordinance building, whose lab Alex Gentry is using as his base of operations.\n\nBut when he enters the building, he's surprised to find the Porcupine waiting for him!&nbsp; In seconds, his location is spotlighted by phosphorescent pellets attracted to the metal of his helmet, and soon, tiny nets spring out of the Porcupine's outfit and blanket Ant-Man.&nbsp; Before the hero can pry himself loose, the Porcupine has picked him up, used tweezers to remove his helmet to avoid contact with his ant friends, and then drops him into a bathtub full of water.\n\nUnable to use his enlarging gas due to the water around him, Hank also finds the side of the tub too slippery to climb.&nbsp; Furthermore, without his helmet, he's unable to contact any ant backup for help.&nbsp; It seems only a matter of time before he grows too tired to continue treading water -- if he doesn't get help soon, he'll <i>drown</i>!&nbsp; Satisfied that Ant-Man can't escape, the Porcupine heads out again.\n\nBack in Hank's lab, Jan finally awakens and seems to have recovered from her brief illness.&nbsp; Finding Hank gone, she wonders what could have drawn him away and finally deduces that he's tracked down the Porcupine and rushed off to confront him.&nbsp; Using the equipment in the lab to contact the ants, she learns of Ant-Man's location in the Army Ordinance building, but the communication with Ant-Man has suddenly ceased.\n\nOrdering the ants to meet her there, the Wasp flies off and discovers the exhausted Ant-Man in the tub.&nbsp; She's arrived just in the nick of time, and soon, at her direction, a group of ants form a chain of bodies that stretch down toward the water that allows Ant-Man to grab onto.&nbsp; He's saved!\n\nNow freed, Ant-Man orders the Wasp to prepare for the inevitable return of the Porcupine.&nbsp; They prepare bags of Porcupine's own liquid cement to stop up the quills in his suit, and the ants help them position the bags.&nbsp; The moment the villain returns, they pop open the bags and coat the costume with the sticky substance, which neutralizes most of his weaponry.&nbsp; With his suit almost completely useless now, the Porcupine takes off, promising that one day he will return!\n\nHank tells Jan he has something for her back at the lab.&nbsp; Jan imagines jewelry or even a <i>ring</i> perhaps?&nbsp; But when Hank hands her a special antibiotic to help her continue to heal she says, \"Henry Pym...I <i>hate</i> you!\"&nbsp; This prompts Hank's response of, \"Like I always say...you can't please a female!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "The villain is window dressing around Jan trying to reach Hank through his fog.\nAn uneven plot buoyed by a few honest and genuinely enjoyable moments.",
  "subtitle": "Overt Love Meets the Oblivious",
  "review_text": "While the new villain of the Porcupine was little more than an Iron Man rip-off, especially as the suit seems to allow its creator to whip up one outlandish gizmo after another (e.g. liquid cement, sleeping gas, hypnotic wheels, phosphorescent pellets, tiny nets, and so on), the story of Alex Gentry ultimately mattered far less than what the issue was actually interested in.\n\nThis was a story that allowed the Wasp to shine, though it could never have happened without her illness, which conveniently removes her from the initial action and forces Hank into a solo confrontation, ultimately putting him in a position where he needs her help to get free.&nbsp; From a plot perspective, it wasn't a brilliant piece of work, being quite <i>pedestrian</i>, in fact.&nbsp; And as I said, the villain of the month was nothing to write home about <i>either</i>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=But <i>again</i>, none of that was really the point.]\n\nWhat Stan seemed to want to emphasize in this outing was the mismatched relationship between Jan and her ever-clueless partner, and in <i>that</i> respect, the story worked (even as its main antagonist did absolutely nothing to make you yearn for his eventual return).\n\n[ASSET: right | SixLegs | width=24%] Jan continues to be obsessed with trying to get Hank to take her seriously and actually <i>desire</i> her, endlessly trying to make him jealous to prompt him toward <i>some </i>kind of action, but the story mostly continues to reinforce the notion he considers her a kid.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This treatment is uneven from one issue to the next, of course, but mostly follows this pattern.)]\n[BR]\n[BR] I actually <i>liked</i> the fact that Hank attributed her obvious affection to the delirium of her illness, and no comment illustrated Jan's true feelings about the ever-so-serious scientist better than the one where she pondered whether Hank would like her better if she had six legs.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And Heck knocks this panel out of the park!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>Excellent</i> dialogue], not to mention a peek into Jan's true emotions for Hank.&nbsp; Moments like these underscore how openly Jan wears her heart on her sleeve, even as Hank remains stubbornly trapped inside his own head, comically oblivious to every <i>bit</i> of it.\n\n[ASSET: left | SimpleDrowning | width=12%] [ASSET: right | ThankGodYoureHere | width=18%] Hank getting captured by the Porcupine was only a necessary plot element to get him into a predicament from which he could not escape alone.&nbsp; It wasn't great, and I question the ease at which the Porcupine overcomes Ant-Man (or his foresight that the hero was about to show up in the first place).\n[BR]\n[BR] Be that as it may, the plight of the tiny hero about to drown in something as simple as a bathtub was actually a novel idea by the writers -- a rare instance where Ant‑Man's scale becomes genuinely <i>threatening</i> rather than merely a gimmick.&nbsp; This maneuvered Jan into position to come to his aid, but also gave voice to her objection about him barking out orders the <i>second</i> he got free.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter his rescue, it was only a matter of time before Hank thought to use the Porcupine's own weapon to gum up the quill tubes.\n\n[ASSET: right | LiquidCement | width=11%] And with only two pages of story left, the Porcupine walks right into the trap laid for him by Ant-Man and the Wasp, thanks in large part to the ever-useful ant army at their disposal.\n[BR]\n[BR] As soon as the Porcupine enters the room, Wasp pops the bags of liquid cement, which coats the Porcupine costume -- and just like that, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(though the Porcupine himself is able to flee, presumably to return in a later issue)].\n\nThere was only one thing left to do, which was to end the issue with yet one more Hank/Jan personal moment, and like any geeky scientist, Hank just can't seem to please his partner, offering her [SPAN: severity=error; text=antibiotics] rather than jewelry, much to Jan's annoyance.\n[ASSET: center | OnlyOneThingLeft | width=67%]\n\nIt's like Hank always says, \"you can't please a female!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's a glib remark, but perfectly in character for Hank as he's written here.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nI thought Don Heck's artwork was a bit better here than in much of his published work.&nbsp; It seemed to have more detail.&nbsp; No inker is credited though, so perhaps this is what it looks like when he does the whole job himself.&nbsp; Anyway, I thought it was quite good, particularly page 8, the one where Jan wishes she had six legs so she could get more of Hank's attention.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe Porcupine seems destined to join the long line of forgettable villains this title keeps producing.\n\nBut his presence evidently served one purpose, which was to help advance the ongoing (and seemingly mismatched) relationship between Hank and Jan.&nbsp; We saw glimpses of this interaction in this month's debut issue of the <i>Avengers</i>, where Jan seemed to be constantly trying to get Hank jealous.\n\nHere, she keeps calling out how nice it is when he pays attention to her, how annoyed with him she gets when he treats her as one to boss around, and how disappointed she was by his present for her at issue's end.&nbsp; These were only small moments in the story, but they were so cleverly sprinkled in by the writers that I never lost interest.&nbsp; In fact, they even provided brief glimmers of light in an issue that looked like it would be nothing other than one more objectionable Ant-Man/Wasp outing.\n\nLet me be clear:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=if the Porcupine ever appears again, it will be unfortunate, because as a reader, I couldn't care less about him.]&nbsp; But this issue gave us the one thing that's unique about these heroes, which is to continue to highlight how Jan is head-over-heels in love with Hank, even though he's too clueless to notice.\n\nIt's not novel, the villain is silly, the plot is largely uninspired, and the execution clunky at times, but reading Jan's reactions to Hank is nevertheless fun.&nbsp; If a moment of delight is all this book can muster, then [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=I'll take it.]",
  "side_note": [
    "It is a little surprising that someone like Hank Pym has designed the lock on a bank vault.&nbsp; I thought he was a <i>biologist</i>.&nbsp; Designing bank vaults seems to be more in line with what Tony Stark might do.&nbsp; It's a small point, and doesn't spoil the story, but I still found it odd."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "The Porcupine continues to pop up here and there in Marvel Comics, never being a top-tier villain, but I noted his introduction in the MCU show <i>She-Hulk</i>, looking remarkably the same as in this origin tale.",
  "highlight": "Porcupine blusters, but it's Jan chasing Hank through his fog that matters."
}